# `heroes probe` in the net (panel 179 item 7; design.md §4.15): the
# formatter's probe over its fixture directories under every family, and
# over the whole tree under the thin one, `multi`, alone.
#
# WHAT A ROW ASSERTS. The probe exits 0, every variant the parser accepts
# holding under both judges, or 2 with exactly the failures a row names as
# an open defect's, each by its file, family and variant; and its report
# reads at least the seeds and, per family, the variants generated and
# parsing the row pins. The floors are the point (the spec-warden's
# condition): a generator that silently made fewer variants would judge
# less and pass, the green run that tested nothing. A `multi` row is
# checked by what the value is as well: that family makes nine variants of
# every seed, so its count is nine times the seeds, whatever the tree holds.
#
# THE FIXTURE ROW JUDGES ONE VARIANT IN TWO. Unstrided it measured 64.65 s,
# 64.79 s and 65.26 s of user time with a compiler built unoptimised, as
# the gate's is (2026-09-28, the load between 2 and 8), past the 60 s panel
# 179 set for an unstrided row, so the row takes `--stride 2`, which judges
# the same variants every run; every one of them is judged by hand before a
# push that touches `selfhost/print/` (`.claude/rules/verification.md`). Its
# directories are the ones a `fmt` row of `suite_surface.hero` reads, and
# the row checks that the report names each, so a formatter fixture added
# there and not to the probe's default is red here. It stays ONE
# invocation, and defect 155's repair measured why (2026-10-02): its stride
# is counted across the run, so its files probed one at a time at
# `--stride 2` judge other variants, of which 3382 `bracket` and 696
# `paren` parse, under the floors of 3395 and 703, and a floor is never
# lowered. It read 48.59 s of user time that day, 40% of the watchdog.
#
# A TREE ROW PROBES ITS FILES ONE AT A TIME (defect 155's second half,
# 2026-10-02). The harness stops any one program at 120 s of wall
# (`shell.WATCHDOG_SECONDS`), and `heroes probe selfhost --family multi` in
# one invocation measured 97.50 s of user time on this Mac (real 104.34, a
# busy machine, the gate's unoptimised compiler) and was stopped at exit 124
# on the CI's Linux arm64 runner (run 36987357726), as it had been in the
# Linux x86-64 container and in the batch gates' parallel passes since
# 2026-10-01; `tests` read 46.81 s and `examples` 14.39 s. A row's cost is
# its tree's lines, between 0.4 and 1.5 ms each in the files measured, so
# every file added brought the limit closer. So a tree row runs `heroes
# probe <file> --family multi` once for each `.hero` file under its root:
# one invocation costs one file, the longest, `suite_records.hero` at 5,138
# lines, 5.87 s, whatever the tree holds, and every seed is still judged.
# The row sums what its files report and holds the sums to its floors. What
# it costs, in instructions retired, which a busy machine's cores do not
# move as they move its user time: 1470.84 billion for the three tree rows
# file by file with their censuses, against 1412.72 billion in one
# invocation each, 4.1% more.
#
# NO FILE FALLS BETWEEN THE INVOCATIONS. The row first asks the probe what
# it reads of the whole root, at the largest stride it takes, which judges
# one variant and counts every seed, refused file and variant (1.36 s for
# `selfhost`); the files probed one at a time must read exactly those three
# counts, so a file the probe reads under the root and the row's walk does
# not, or one the walk adds, is red.
#
# KNOWN FAILURES, each an open defect's and named with it: the row is red
# when one stops failing, which is the day its entry goes, as well as when
# anything else fails.

use report
use floors
use shell
use strings
use suite_surface

record Floor
    family: str
    generated: i64
    parsing: i64

record Row
    name: str
    words: [str]
    seeds: i64
    floors: [Floor]
    known: [str]

# A row over a tree: every `.hero` file under `root` probed alone under
# `floor.family`, the counts summed.
record Tree
    name: str
    root: str
    seeds: i64
    floor: Floor
    known: [str]

# What a tree's files read, summed, the failures they name, and each run
# no row allows, told as it came.
record Sum
    seeds: i64
    refused: i64
    generated: i64
    parsing: i64
    named: [str]
    broken: [str]

# The stride of the census: the largest the probe takes, its `counted`
# refusing an eighth digit. `--stride` judges one variant in that many and
# counts every one, so under it the probe parses the whole root, judges its
# first variant and says how many seeds, refused files and variants the
# root holds.
constant CENSUS_STRIDE: str
    "9999999"

# The probe's own words when the one file it is given does not parse
# (`selfhost/cli/probe.hero`, at exit 1): such a file is no seed, as it is
# under a directory, where the probe names it on a `note:` line.
constant REFUSING: str
    "error: refusing to probe a file with diagnostics"

# How many of a tree's runs no row allows a failure shows.
constant SHOWN: i64
    3

# No failure is known, since panel 181's landing (2026-09-28). Defect 116's
# shape in `comments107/margin.hero`, a comment on a line of its own inside a
# statement continued at its own margin, was the one: seven variants in the
# fixtures row and three in the tests row, refused by the guard at exit 2.
# The statement no longer parses, so the file is no seed and its failures
# went with it, measured: 0 variants refused by the guard in any row. A new
# failure names its defect here.
#
# **Eight, defect 124's, since `patterns125` became a seed** (2026-09-28, the
# repair of defect 125). The `paren` family wraps a negative pattern's
# literal in the author's parentheses with a comment inside, `-(  # c` over
# `1)`, and the parser reads it as a pattern, since after a `-` it takes any
# unary operand where spec § 8 takes a literal; `fmt` prints `-1` and the
# guard refuses to drop the comment. Fifteen variants unstrided, all `paren`
# and all on a pattern's line, eight of them in the row's stride. Repairing
# 124 takes the shape out of the parser, and each entry out of this row.
#
# **None again since defect 124's repair** (2026-09-28): a pattern takes one
# literal after its sign, so `-(  # c` over `1)` no longer parses and is no
# variant the guard judges. Measured: 0 refused by the guard in the fixtures
# row, strided and unstrided, and `paren`'s parsing count 711 -> 703 in the
# stride (1398 -> 1383 unstrided), the fifteen and nothing else.
function fixture_row() -> Row
    # Measured 2026-09-28 at panel 181's landing, which added
    # `surface-fixtures/continuation181/` (two seeds, two files refused). The
    # floors measured again the same day with `patterns125/negative.hero`,
    # the 65th seed (defect 125).
    fixtures: [Floor] = [
        Floor(family: "single", generated: 6479, parsing: 3240)
        Floor(family: "multi", generated: 585, parsing: 293)
        Floor(family: "bracket", generated: 9120, parsing: 3395)
        Floor(family: "paren", generated: 2220, parsing: 703)
        Floor(family: "ends", generated: 518, parsing: 259)
        Floor(family: "columns", generated: 5500, parsing: 2750)
        Floor(family: "texts", generated: 3254, parsing: 1627)
    ]
    none: [str] = []
    return Row(
        name: "the fixtures, every family",
        words: ["probe", "--stride", "2"],
        seeds: 65,
        floors: fixtures,
        known: none
    )

function trees() -> [Tree]
    # Measured 2026-09-28 at panel 181's landing, which added its four lexer
    # modules to selfhost and its goldens to tests. The selfhost row raised
    # 2026-10-02 at lane literals' batch gate, to what the suite asked:
    # `escape_codes.hero` took selfhost from 355 modules to 356, past 284 by
    # more than a quarter, and its variants to 3204. The tests row raised
    # 2026-10-02 at the round's gate of lanes recovery-b8, land186 and h158,
    # to what the suite asked: the round's 53 new `.hero` files under `tests/`
    # took its seeds to 799, past 634 by more than a quarter, and its
    # variants to 7191.
    # Both rows raised 2026-10-07 at batch 13's gate, to what the suite asked:
    # selfhost to 469 seeds and 4221 variants, tests to 1100 and 9900, the
    # batch's modules and cases taking each past its floor by more than a quarter.
    # The selfhost row raised 2026-10-10 at batch 18's gate, to what the suite
    # asked: 599 seeds and 5391 variants, past 469 and 4221 by more than a quarter.
    none: [str] = []
    return [
        Tree(name: "selfhost, multi", root: "selfhost", seeds: 599, floor: Floor(family: "multi", generated: 5391, parsing: 5391), known: none)
        Tree(name: "tests, multi", root: "tests", seeds: 1100, floor: Floor(family: "multi", generated: 9900, parsing: 9900), known: none)
        Tree(name: "examples, multi", root: "examples", seeds: 120, floor: Floor(family: "multi", generated: 1080, parsing: 1080), known: none)
    ]

function run_all(scratch: str, compiler: str) -> report.Report?
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()
    row = fixture_row()
    words: [str] @ [compiler]

    for word in row.words
        words @ words.push(word)

    ran = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: words)?
    judged(@r, row: row, ran: ran)

    # Every directory a `fmt` row reads is one the fixtures' row probed.
    for dir in fixture_directories()
        if !strings.contains(text: ran.out, needle: "\n  " + dir + ": ")
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: "probe/fixtures",
                detail: "  `heroes probe`'s default does not read " + dir + ", which a `fmt` row of the surface suite reads: add it to `selfhost/cli/probe.hero`'s FIXTURES"
            )

    for tree in trees()
        family = tree.floor.family
        census = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "probe", tree.root, "--family", family, "--stride", CENSUS_STRIDE])?
        files = shell.found_under(dir: tree.root, suffix: ".hero", reference: "")?
        none: [str] = []
        sum: Sum @ Sum(seeds: 0, refused: 0, generated: 0, parsing: 0, named: none, broken: none)

        for file in files
            one = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "probe", file, "--family", family])?
            sum @ added(sum, file: file, family: family, ran: one)

        summed(@r, tree: tree, census: census, sum: sum)

    return ok(r)

# What one row's run says, against what the row pins.
function judged(@r: report.Report, row: Row, ran: shell.Run)
    label = "probe/" + row.name
    named = failures(ran.err)
    want: i64 @ 0

    if row.known.len() > 0
        want @ 2

    if ran.code != want
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: label,
            detail: "  exited " + ran.code.to_str() + ", not " + want.to_str() + ":\n" + head(ran.err)
        )
        return

    for one in named
        if !listed(row.known, key: one)
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: label,
                detail: "  a failure no open defect names: " + one + "\n" + head(ran.err)
            )
            return

    for one in row.known
        if !listed(named, key: one)
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: label,
                detail: "  " + one + " no longer fails: its defect's repair takes it out of this row's known failures"
            )
            return

    seeds = seeds_of(ran.out)
    floors.check(@r, label: label + "/seeds", found: seeds, floor: row.seeds, what: "seeds")

    for floor in row.floors
        cells = tally(text: ran.out, family: floor.family)

        if cells.len() != 7
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: label,
                detail: "  the report has no row for " + floor.family + ":\n" + ran.out
            )
            continue
        generated = number(cells[1])
        parsing = number(cells[3])
        floors.check(
            @r,
            label: label + "/" + floor.family,
            found: generated,
            floor: floor.generated,
            what: "variants generated"
        )
        floors.check(
            @r,
            label: label + "/" + floor.family + "/parsing",
            found: parsing,
            floor: floor.parsing,
            what: "variants parsing"
        )

        # A fact about the value: `multi` rewrites each seed nine ways.
        if floor.family == "multi" && row.floors.len() == 1 && generated != 9 * seeds
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: label,
                detail: "  `multi` made " + generated.to_str() + " variants of " + seeds.to_str() + " seeds, not nine of each"
            )

## A tree, one file at a time

# What one file's run adds to its tree's sum: a seed and its variants, a
# file the parser refuses, the failures it names, or a run no row allows.
# Exit 1 is a refusal only with the probe's own words for one: anything
# else at 1, and every exit but 0 and a 2 naming its failures, is kept to
# be told, the watchdog's 124 among them.
function added(sum: Sum, file: str, family: str, ran: shell.Run) -> Sum
    out: Sum @ sum

    if ran.code == 1 && strings.contains(text: ran.err, needle: REFUSING)
        out.refused @ out.refused + 1
        return out
    named = failures(ran.err)

    if ran.code != 0 && (ran.code != 2 || named.len() == 0)
        out.broken @ out.broken.push("  `" + file + "` exited " + ran.code.to_str() + ":\n" + head(ran.err))
        return out
    cells = tally(text: ran.out, family: family)

    if cells.len() != 7
        out.broken @ out.broken.push("  `" + file + "`: the report has no row for " + family + ":\n" + ran.out)
        return out
    out.seeds @ out.seeds + seeds_of(ran.out)
    out.refused @ out.refused + refused_of(ran.out)
    out.generated @ out.generated + number(cells[1])
    out.parsing @ out.parsing + number(cells[3])

    for one in named
        out.named @ out.named.push(one)

    return out

# What a tree's files read together, against what the probe reads of its
# root and what the row pins.
function summed(@r: report.Report, tree: Tree, census: shell.Run, sum: Sum)
    label = "probe/" + tree.name
    family = tree.floor.family

    # The census judges one variant, so a 2 is a known failure that happens
    # to be the root's first; its files' runs say whether it is known.
    if census.code != 0 && census.code != 2
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: label,
            detail: "  the census, `heroes probe " + tree.root + " --family " + family + " --stride " + CENSUS_STRIDE + "`, exited " + census.code.to_str() + ":\n" + head(census.err)
        )
        return

    if sum.broken.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: label,
            detail: shown(sum.broken) + "\n  (" + sum.broken.len().to_str() + " of the files under " + tree.root + " exited as no row allows)"
        )
        return

    for one in sum.named
        if !listed(tree.known, key: one)
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: label, detail: "  a failure no open defect names: " + one)
            return

    for one in tree.known
        if !listed(sum.named, key: one)
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: label,
                detail: "  " + one + " no longer fails: its defect's repair takes it out of this row's known failures"
            )
            return

    cells = tally(text: census.out, family: family)

    if cells.len() != 7
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: label,
            detail: "  the census's report has no row for " + family + ":\n" + census.out
        )
        return
    seeds = seeds_of(census.out)
    refused = refused_of(census.out)
    generated = number(cells[1])

    if sum.seeds != seeds || sum.refused != refused || sum.generated != generated
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: label + "/covered",
            detail: "  the files under " + tree.root + ", one at a time, read " + counted(seeds: sum.seeds, refused: sum.refused, generated: sum.generated) + ", and `heroes probe " + tree.root + "` reads " + counted(seeds: seeds, refused: refused, generated: generated) + ": a file it reads fell between the runs, or a run read one it does not"
        )
        return
    report.keep_pass(@r)
    floors.check(@r, label: label + "/seeds", found: sum.seeds, floor: tree.seeds, what: "seeds")
    floors.check(
        @r,
        label: label + "/" + family,
        found: sum.generated,
        floor: tree.floor.generated,
        what: "variants generated"
    )
    floors.check(
        @r,
        label: label + "/" + family + "/parsing",
        found: sum.parsing,
        floor: tree.floor.parsing,
        what: "variants parsing"
    )

    # A fact about the value: `multi` rewrites each seed nine ways.
    if family == "multi" && sum.generated != 9 * sum.seeds
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: label,
            detail: "  `multi` made " + sum.generated.to_str() + " variants of " + sum.seeds.to_str() + " seeds, not nine of each"
        )

function counted(seeds: i64, refused: i64, generated: i64) -> str
    return seeds.to_str() + " seeds, " + refused.to_str() + " files the parser refuses and " + generated.to_str() + " variants"

# The first of a tree's runs no row allows, and how many more there were.
function shown(broken: [str]) -> str
    if broken.len() <= SHOWN
        return broken.join("\n")
    return broken.slice(from: 0, to: SHOWN).join("\n") + "\n  and " + (broken.len() - SHOWN).to_str() + " more"

## Reading the probe's report

# Every failure the report names, as `<file> <family> <variant>`: the first
# is written `, family F, variant V,` after its file, the next ones `F V,`.
function failures(err: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    for line in strings.lines(err)
        first = strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "error: the first, `")

        if !first && !strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "    `")
            continue
        parts = strings.split_text(text: line, separator: "`")

        if parts.len() < 3
            continue
        pieces = strings.split_text(text: parts[2], separator: ",")
        found: [str] @ [parts[1]]

        if first && pieces.len() > 2
            for word in strings.words(pieces[1] + " " + pieces[2])
                if word != "family" && word != "variant"
                    found @ found.push(word)
        else if !first
            for word in strings.words(pieces[0])
                found @ found.push(word)

        out @ out.push(found.join(" "))

    return out

# The report's first line, `probe: S seeds, and R files the parser
# refuses`, read for S and for R.
function seeds_of(out: str) -> i64
    words = strings.words(out)

    if words.len() < 2 || words[0] != "probe:"
        return 0
    return number(words[1])

function refused_of(out: str) -> i64
    words = strings.words(out)

    if words.len() < 5 || words[0] != "probe:" || words[3] != "and"
        return -1
    return number(words[4])

# The cells of the report's row for `family`, the family first.
function tally(text: str, family: str) -> [str]
    for line in strings.lines(text)
        if strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "| " + family + " | ")
            cells: [str] @ []

            for cell in strings.split_text(text: line, separator: "|")
                if strings.trimmed(cell).len() > 0
                    cells @ cells.push(strings.trimmed(cell))

            return cells

    return []

function number(text: str) -> i64
    return strings.to_number(text).default(-1)

function listed(entries: [str], key: str) -> bool
    for one in entries
        if one == key
            return true

    return false

function head(text: str) -> str
    lines = strings.lines(text)

    if lines.len() > 12
        return lines.slice(from: 0, to: 12).join("\n") + "\n  ..."
    return text

# The directories the surface suite's `fmt` rows read.
function fixture_directories() -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    tables = [
        suite_surface.argv_probes()
        suite_surface.help_probes()
        suite_surface.render_probes()
        suite_surface.ffi_probes()
        suite_surface.verb_probes()
        suite_surface.bracket_probes()
    ]

    for table in tables
        for p in table
            words = strings.words(p.argv)

            if words.len() < 2 || words[0] != "fmt" || !strings.starts_with(text: words[1], prefix: "tests/golden/surface-fixtures/")
                continue
            dir = directory_of(words[1])

            if !listed(out, key: dir)
                out @ out.push(dir)

    return out

function directory_of(path: str) -> str
    at: i64 @ path.len() - 1

    while at > 0 && path[at] != '/'
        at @ at - 1

    return path.slice(from: 0, to: at)

## Tests

test "a report's failures, seeds and rows are read as the probe writes them"
    err = "error: `fmt` failed 2 of 9 variants the parser accepts: 2 refused by its own guard, 0 by the independent reader\nerror: the first, `a/b.hero`, family single, variant O14_0, the guard: it moved\n  the variant leaves the file at its line 14:\n  the next:\n    `a/b.hero` multi ALLO, the guard: it moved\nerror: this is a compiler bug, and no file was changed"
    assert failures(err) == ["a/b.hero single O14_0", "a/b.hero multi ALLO"]
    out = "probe: 3 seeds, and 1 files the parser refuses\n  x: 3 seeds\n\n| family | generated | judged | parsing | held | refused by the guard | refused by the reader |\n| multi | 27 | 27 | 27 | 27 | 0 | 0 |\n"
    assert seeds_of(out) == 3
    assert refused_of(out) == 1
    assert refused_of("probe: 3 seeds") == -1
    assert tally(text: out, family: "multi") == ["multi", "27", "27", "27", "27", "0", "0"]
    assert tally(text: out, family: "single") == []
    assert directory_of("tests/golden/surface-fixtures/comments101/a.hero") == "tests/golden/surface-fixtures/comments101"
    assert fixture_directories().len() >= 9

test "a row is red on an exit it does not expect, a failure no defect names, and a known one that holds"
    row = fixture_row()
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()
    judged(
        @r,
        row: row,
        ran: shell.Run(code: 2, out: "", err: "error: the first, `x.hero`, family multi, variant ALLO, the guard: it moved", started: true, why: 0)
    )
    assert r.failed == 1
    known = Row(name: "known", words: row.words, seeds: row.seeds, floors: row.floors, known: ["x.hero multi ALLO"])
    k: report.Report @ report.empty()
    judged(@k, row: known, ran: shell.Run(code: 2, out: "", err: "", started: true, why: 0))
    assert k.failed == 1

# A file's run, as the probe writes it: `seeds` seeds of nine variants.
function file_run(code: i64, seeds: i64, err: str) -> shell.Run
    variants = (9 * seeds).to_str()
    out = "probe: " + seeds.to_str() + " seeds, and 0 files the parser refuses\n  f.hero: " + seeds.to_str() + " seeds\n\n| family | generated | judged | parsing | held | refused by the guard | refused by the reader |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| multi | " + variants + " | " + variants + " | " + variants + " | " + variants + " | 0 | 0 |\n"
    return shell.Run(code: code, out: out, err: err, started: true, why: 0)

function no_sum() -> Sum
    none: [str] = []
    return Sum(seeds: 0, refused: 0, generated: 0, parsing: 0, named: none, broken: none)

test "a file's run adds a seed, a file the parser refuses, or a run no row allows"
    sum: Sum @ added(no_sum(), file: "a.hero", family: "multi", ran: file_run(code: 0, seeds: 1, err: ""))
    sum @ added(
        sum,
        file: "b.hero",
        family: "multi",
        ran: shell.Run(code: 1, out: "", err: "a.hero:1:1: error\n" + REFUSING, started: true, why: 0)
    )
    assert sum.seeds == 1
    assert sum.refused == 1
    assert sum.generated == 9
    assert sum.parsing == 9
    assert sum.broken.len() == 0

    # The watchdog's 124, a 1 the probe does not explain as a refusal, and a
    # 2 that names no failure are each kept, by the file they came from.
    sum @ added(
        sum,
        file: "c.hero",
        family: "multi",
        ran: shell.Run(code: 124, out: "", err: "", started: true, why: 0)
    )
    sum @ added(
        sum,
        file: "d.hero",
        family: "multi",
        ran: shell.Run(code: 1, out: "", err: "error: something else", started: true, why: 0)
    )
    sum @ added(
        sum,
        file: "e.hero",
        family: "multi",
        ran: shell.Run(code: 2, out: "", err: "error: cannot read `e.hero`", started: true, why: 0)
    )
    assert sum.broken.len() == 3
    assert strings.starts_with(text: sum.broken[0], prefix: "  `c.hero` exited 124")
    assert sum.seeds == 1

    # A 2 naming its failures adds them, to be held to the row's known ones.
    failing = file_run(
        code: 2,
        seeds: 1,
        err: "error: the first, `f.hero`, family multi, variant ALLO, the guard: it moved"
    )
    sum @ added(sum, file: "f.hero", family: "multi", ran: failing)
    assert sum.named == ["f.hero multi ALLO"]
    assert sum.seeds == 2

test "a tree is red when its files do not read what the probe reads of its root"
    tree = Tree(name: "t", root: "t", seeds: 2, floor: Floor(family: "multi", generated: 18, parsing: 18), known: [])
    census = file_run(code: 0, seeds: 2, err: "")
    whole: Sum @ added(no_sum(), file: "a.hero", family: "multi", ran: file_run(code: 0, seeds: 1, err: ""))
    whole @ added(whole, file: "b.hero", family: "multi", ran: file_run(code: 0, seeds: 1, err: ""))
    green: report.Report @ report.empty()
    summed(@green, tree: tree, census: census, sum: whole)
    assert green.failed == 0
    assert green.passed == 4

    # One file short of the root: the census says so, whatever the floors.
    short = added(no_sum(), file: "a.hero", family: "multi", ran: file_run(code: 0, seeds: 1, err: ""))
    low = Tree(name: "t", root: "t", seeds: 1, floor: Floor(family: "multi", generated: 9, parsing: 9), known: [])
    missed: report.Report @ report.empty()
    summed(@missed, tree: low, census: census, sum: short)
    assert missed.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: missed.failures[0], needle: "FAIL probe/t/covered")

    # A census the watchdog stopped, and a file's run no row allows.
    stopped: report.Report @ report.empty()
    summed(@stopped, tree: tree, census: shell.Run(code: 124, out: "", err: "", started: true, why: 0), sum: whole)
    assert stopped.failed == 1
    killed = added(
        whole,
        file: "c.hero",
        family: "multi",
        ran: shell.Run(code: 124, out: "", err: "", started: true, why: 0)
    )
    broken: report.Report @ report.empty()
    summed(@broken, tree: tree, census: census, sum: killed)
    assert broken.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: broken.failures[0], needle: "`c.hero` exited 124")

test "a tree's failures are held to the ones its row names as known"
    census = file_run(code: 0, seeds: 1, err: "")
    failing = added(
        no_sum(),
        file: "f.hero",
        family: "multi",
        ran: file_run(code: 2, seeds: 1, err: "error: the first, `f.hero`, family multi, variant ALLO, the guard: it moved")
    )
    floor = Floor(family: "multi", generated: 9, parsing: 9)
    unknown: report.Report @ report.empty()
    summed(@unknown, tree: Tree(name: "t", root: "t", seeds: 1, floor: floor, known: []), census: census, sum: failing)
    assert unknown.failed == 1
    named: report.Report @ report.empty()
    summed(
        @named,
        tree: Tree(name: "t", root: "t", seeds: 1, floor: floor, known: ["f.hero multi ALLO"]),
        census: census,
        sum: failing
    )
    assert named.failed == 0
    gone: report.Report @ report.empty()
    clean = added(no_sum(), file: "f.hero", family: "multi", ran: file_run(code: 0, seeds: 1, err: ""))
    summed(
        @gone,
        tree: Tree(name: "t", root: "t", seeds: 1, floor: floor, known: ["f.hero multi ALLO"]),
        census: census,
        sum: clean
    )
    assert gone.failed == 1
